2.2 Recurrent Memory Architectures

Recurrent Memory Transformer (RMT) introduces memory tokens that propagate information across segments, enabling long-range dependencies without storing an entire sequence directly. However, memory remains represented as token-level embeddings.

2.3 Compressive Memory

Infini-Attention introduces compressive memory structures that summarize historical information while maintaining bounded computation. Nevertheless, memory remains derived from compressed token representations.

2.4 Neural Memory

Titans introduces a neural long-term memory module capable of memorization during inference and maintains a distinction between attention-based short-term memory and neural long-term memory.

Despite significant advances, existing methods continue to represent memory as either:

token states,
hidden states,
compressed token summaries,
retrieved token memories.

No dominant architecture currently treats adaptive parameters themselves as the primary memory substrate.

