10. Experimental Predictions

PMA produces several testable hypotheses.

H1

Adaptive parameter memory can achieve retrieval performance comparable to token retention.

H2

Memory complexity remains bounded as context length increases.

H3

Reasoning performance degrades more slowly than attention-based architectures under extreme context lengths.

H4

Memory quality depends primarily on parameter adaptation quality rather than retained token count.

11. Limitations

This work is currently a theoretical framework.

Several challenges remain:

Catastrophic forgetting.
Stability of continual updates.
Information capacity limits.
Parameter interference.
Convergence guarantees.

Additionally, token retention may preserve exact information unavailable under lossy parameter compression.

Whether adaptive parameters can asymptotically approach token memory remains an open empirical question.

12. Conclusion

This paper introduces Parametric Memory Alignment (PMA), a new framework built upon a simple but fundamental proposition:

Memory should be represented by adaptive parameters rather than preserved tokens.

Instead of extending context windows or improving token storage mechanisms, PMA reframes long-context reasoning as a continual parameter adaptation problem.

The resulting Hypercycle Transformer architecture separates reasoning from memory formation and proposes a parameter-centric alternative to token-centric memory systems.

If validated empirically, PMA suggests a future direction in which scaling intelligence depends less on retaining ever larger contexts and more on constructing increasingly effective adaptive memory mechanisms.

